1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,152
Take a roast off the heat, and its centre
keeps getting hotter.

2
00:00:03,152 --> 00:00:07,794
Resting lets two physical processes finish
after the heat is gone: heat keeps

3
00:00:07,794 --> 00:00:12,435
travelling inward from the hot outer
layers, so the centre continues to cook,

4
00:00:12,435 --> 00:00:16,896
and the meat cools enough that less of its
liquid runs out when it is cut.

5
00:00:16,896 --> 00:00:22,334
The USDA's temperature guidance for whole
cuts of beef, pork, veal and lamb pairs

6
00:00:22,334 --> 00:00:27,503
145 degrees Fahrenheit with a 3-minute
rest, because the temperature holds or

7
00:00:27,503 --> 00:00:29,115
climbs during that time.

8
00:00:29,115 --> 00:00:31,252
That figure settles the heat question.

9
00:00:31,252 --> 00:00:35,640
It does not settle the question most
people mean, which is about the puddle on

10
00:00:35,640 --> 00:00:36,203
the board.

11
00:00:36,203 --> 00:00:38,779
Why does meat need to rest after cooking?

12
00:00:38,779 --> 00:00:44,587
Start with what the pan or oven actually
does. Heat enters at the surface.

13
00:00:44,587 --> 00:00:49,093
It travels inward by conduction, with each
layer of meat warming the next.

14
00:00:49,093 --> 00:00:53,812
That process is slow, because meat is
mostly water and protein, and neither

15
00:00:53,812 --> 00:00:55,008
moves heat quickly.

16
00:00:55,008 --> 00:00:59,515
So at any moment during cooking, a thick
roast is not one temperature.

17
00:00:59,515 --> 00:01:04,566
It has hot outer layers, a cooler centre,
and a slope of temperatures running

18
00:01:04,566 --> 00:01:05,419
between them.

19
00:01:05,419 --> 00:01:08,691
When you lift it out, nothing feeds the
outside any more.

20
00:01:08,691 --> 00:01:12,709
The heat already stored in those outer
layers does not vanish, though.

21
00:01:12,709 --> 00:01:14,575
Some of it escapes into the air.

22
00:01:14,575 --> 00:01:19,356
The rest keeps flowing the only other way
it can, inward and down the slope toward

23
00:01:19,356 --> 00:01:20,405
the cooler centre.

24
00:01:20,405 --> 00:01:24,016
That is why a roast keeps cooking after it
leaves the heat.

25
00:01:24,016 --> 00:01:26,162
You do not have to take this on trust.

26
00:01:26,162 --> 00:01:30,735
The instrument is a probe thermometer in
the centre, left in place after the meat

27
00:01:30,735 --> 00:01:31,808
comes off the heat.

28
00:01:31,808 --> 00:01:36,559
For a while, the reading keeps rising off
the heat, with no flame and no oven

29
00:01:36,559 --> 00:01:37,115
involved.

30
00:01:37,115 --> 00:01:42,219
Then it levels off, and then it falls.
Cooks call the rising part carryover

31
00:01:42,219 --> 00:01:44,579
The shape of that curve is the evidence.

32
00:01:44,579 --> 00:01:49,357
After the source is removed, the only heat
that can warm the centre is heat still

33
00:01:49,357 --> 00:01:51,717
travelling inward from the outer layers.

34
00:01:51,717 --> 00:01:55,866
Cut a roast in half and a thermal camera
shows the same pattern in colour: a hot

35
00:01:55,866 --> 00:01:57,525
rim fading into a cooler middle.

36
00:01:57,525 --> 00:02:02,689
How much the centre rises is where the
honest answer becomes a range with no

37
00:02:02,689 --> 00:02:03,504
fixed edges.

38
00:02:03,504 --> 00:02:08,523
It depends on the size of the cut, how hot
the outside was, and how long it cooked.

39
00:02:08,523 --> 00:02:13,865
A large roast from a hot oven stores a
great deal of heat in its outer layers and

40
00:02:13,865 --> 00:02:15,184
can rise noticeably.

41
00:02:15,184 --> 00:02:19,691
A thin steak stores little heat and cools
from both faces almost at once.

42
00:02:19,691 --> 00:02:24,393
Estimates vary, and different figures get
quoted for different cuts, so this script

43
00:02:24,393 --> 00:02:25,413
will not pick one.

44
00:02:25,413 --> 00:02:28,139
The mechanism, though, does not change.

45
00:02:28,139 --> 00:02:33,312
Carryover is heat already inside moving
inward, not new heat from the pan.

46
00:02:33,312 --> 00:02:38,245
Carryover is also the reason the USDA
guidance includes a rest at all.

47
00:02:38,245 --> 00:02:42,575
The guidance describes its 3-minute rest
as the time after removal when the

48
00:02:42,575 --> 00:02:45,461
temperature remains constant or continues
to rise.

49
00:02:45,461 --> 00:02:50,534
That statement is about temperature and
time, and this episode quotes it only to

50
00:02:50,534 --> 00:02:51,803
explain the physics.

51
00:02:51,803 --> 00:02:56,171
It is not a cooking instruction from this
channel. The point here is narrower.

52
00:02:56,171 --> 00:03:01,115
An official body treats the minutes after
cooking as part of the cooking, because

53
00:03:01,115 --> 00:03:03,312
the thermometer shows that they are.

54
00:03:03,312 --> 00:03:05,241
That's the feeling I'm chasing.

55
00:03:05,241 --> 00:03:10,219
Not collecting another fact — realising
the model in my head was incomplete, and

56
00:03:10,219 --> 00:03:11,712
now I have a better one.

57
00:03:11,712 --> 00:03:12,944
Now for the juice.

58
00:03:12,944 --> 00:03:18,284
Raw muscle is a bundle of long fibres,
each one packed with protein filaments,

59
00:03:18,284 --> 00:03:22,117
and most of its weight is water held in
and around them.

60
00:03:22,117 --> 00:03:25,781
As the meat heats, proteins in the fibres
contract.

61
00:03:25,781 --> 00:03:30,595
They unfold, tangle and tighten, and the
fibres shorten and narrow.

62
00:03:30,595 --> 00:03:34,781
Water is squeezed out, the way water
leaves a sponge as you wring it.

63
00:03:34,781 --> 00:03:39,080
The connective tissue shrinks as well,
wrapped around the bundles and tightening

64
00:03:39,080 --> 00:03:39,725
the squeeze.

65
00:03:39,725 --> 00:03:45,043
The released liquid collects between the
fibres, in channels it did not fill

66
00:03:45,043 --> 00:03:48,212
Some of it reaches the surface and
evaporates or drips.

67
00:03:48,212 --> 00:03:51,728
Some is still in the meat, loose, when you
pick up the knife.

68
00:03:51,728 --> 00:03:56,779
You cannot see water inside a steak, so
this has to be measured indirectly.

69
00:03:56,779 --> 00:04:01,586
One tool is low-field NMR, short for
nuclear magnetic resonance.

70
00:04:01,586 --> 00:04:06,469
It uses the same physics as medical
scanners, at a smaller scale.

71
00:04:06,469 --> 00:04:11,363
It listens to hydrogen in water molecules
and reports how freely that water moves.

72
00:04:11,363 --> 00:04:15,627
Water bound tightly to protein, water
trapped inside the fibres and water

73
00:04:15,627 --> 00:04:18,899
sitting loose between them each give a
different signal.

74
00:04:18,899 --> 00:04:23,635
Meat scientists publishing in journals
such as the Journal of Agricultural and

75
00:04:23,635 --> 00:04:28,675
Food Chemistry, from the American Chemical
Society, have used it to follow cooking.

76
00:04:28,675 --> 00:04:33,691
As the meat heats, the signal from water
inside the fibres shrinks and the signal

77
00:04:33,691 --> 00:04:35,549
from water between them grows.

78
00:04:35,549 --> 00:04:40,114
In other words, heating shifts water
toward the free fraction, out of the

79
00:04:40,114 --> 00:04:41,677
fibres and into the gaps.

80
00:04:41,677 --> 00:04:43,553
This is mainstream science.

81
00:04:43,553 --> 00:04:48,625
Researchers at the USDA Agricultural
Research Service study meat quality,

82
00:04:48,625 --> 00:04:51,891
including how much water a cut holds and
loses.

83
00:04:51,891 --> 00:04:57,080
The Institute of Food Technologists
publishes widely on how heat changes food.

84
00:04:57,080 --> 00:05:01,843
Across that literature the direction is
consistent: more heat means more

85
00:05:01,843 --> 00:05:05,747
contraction, and more contraction pushes
more liquid loose.

86
00:05:05,747 --> 00:05:08,549
The old kitchen explanation gets that part
right.

87
00:05:08,549 --> 00:05:10,723
It goes wrong about what happens next.

88
00:05:10,723 --> 00:05:13,093
The familiar version goes like this.

89
00:05:13,093 --> 00:05:18,428
A hot sear seals the surface, the heat
drives the juice inward to the centre, and

90
00:05:18,428 --> 00:05:23,037
resting lets it spread back out to the
edges so the slice stays moist.

91
00:05:23,037 --> 00:05:27,152
Each step sounds physical, but few of them
survive measurement.

92
00:05:27,152 --> 00:05:29,176
A seared surface is not a seal.

93
00:05:29,176 --> 00:05:34,095
When meat seared first is weighed against
meat that was not, the seared piece does

94
00:05:34,095 --> 00:05:35,475
not reliably lose less.

95
00:05:35,475 --> 00:05:37,417
You can hear the answer too.

96
00:05:37,417 --> 00:05:42,691
A searing steak keeps sizzling, and that
sizzle is water reaching hot metal.

97
00:05:42,691 --> 00:05:46,781
There is also little direct evidence that
juice gets herded into the centre.

98
00:05:46,781 --> 00:05:51,774
Squeezing happens wherever the meat is hot
enough, which is everywhere, and it is

99
00:05:51,774 --> 00:05:53,315
strongest at the outside.

100
00:05:53,315 --> 00:05:57,550
The evidence supports liquid squeezed
loose throughout the meat, not juice

101
00:05:57,550 --> 00:05:58,867
herded into the centre.

102
00:05:58,867 --> 00:06:01,007
So the popular story scores unevenly.

103
00:06:01,007 --> 00:06:04,536
The claim that liquid is squeezed loose by
heat is supported.

104
00:06:04,536 --> 00:06:07,428
The claim that a sear seals the surface is
not.

105
00:06:07,428 --> 00:06:12,473
The claim that juice is herded into the
centre and flows back is unproven at best,

106
00:06:12,473 --> 00:06:15,549
and nothing else in the explanation
depends on it.

107
00:06:15,549 --> 00:06:17,370
That last point matters most.

108
00:06:17,370 --> 00:06:22,328
If the juice is not migrating, resting
cannot be about letting it migrate home.

109
00:06:22,328 --> 00:06:26,632
Something else has to explain the real
difference cooks see on the board.

110
00:06:26,632 --> 00:06:31,821
The least glamorous instrument in any lab
settles most of this. It is a scale.

111
00:06:31,821 --> 00:06:37,171
Meat scientists call the result cooking
loss, and the method fits in a breath.

112
00:06:37,171 --> 00:06:41,133
Weigh the raw meat. Weigh it after
cooking. Weigh it after resting.

113
00:06:41,133 --> 00:06:43,975
Then cut it and weigh what runs onto the
board.

114
00:06:43,975 --> 00:06:48,751
Do that for pieces sliced at once and
pieces left to rest, and you have numbers

115
00:06:48,751 --> 00:06:50,141
instead of impressions.

116
00:06:50,141 --> 00:06:52,197
Everyone notices the liquid on the board.

117
00:06:52,197 --> 00:06:55,405
The earlier weighings tell you where the
rest of the water went.

118
00:06:55,405 --> 00:07:00,413
When cooks and researchers run comparisons
like this, the results point one way.

119
00:07:00,413 --> 00:07:04,856
Meat sliced straight away sheds more onto
the board than meat that has rested.

120
00:07:04,856 --> 00:07:09,058
That difference is real. Resting meat is
not sealed either, though.

121
00:07:09,058 --> 00:07:13,949
While it sits, it stays hot, keeps
steaming and drips a little onto the

122
00:07:13,949 --> 00:07:17,656
Some of the water that would have reached
the board goes into the air instead.

123
00:07:17,656 --> 00:07:22,334
As a result, the total loss of a rested
piece is often closer to that of an

124
00:07:22,334 --> 00:07:24,829
unrested piece than the puddle suggests.

125
00:07:24,829 --> 00:07:29,316
How close depends on the cut and the
conditions, and published comparisons do

126
00:07:29,316 --> 00:07:31,005
not agree on a single figure.

127
00:07:31,005 --> 00:07:34,851
Resting shrinks the puddle more clearly
than it shrinks the total.

128
00:07:34,851 --> 00:07:37,551
So why does less run out of rested meat?

129
00:07:37,551 --> 00:07:41,128
The best-supported answer concerns the
liquid itself.

130
00:07:41,128 --> 00:07:43,065
Meat juice is not plain water.

131
00:07:43,065 --> 00:07:47,779
It carries dissolved proteins, along with
gelatin from connective tissue.

132
00:07:47,779 --> 00:07:52,725
Its viscosity, meaning how strongly a
liquid resists flowing, rises as it cools,

133
00:07:52,725 --> 00:07:56,435
and gelatin in particular thickens as the
temperature drops.

134
00:07:56,435 --> 00:08:00,152
Viscosity is a property of the liquid, not
the amount of it.

135
00:08:00,152 --> 00:08:04,637
The same volume can pour freely when hot
and creep when cooler.

136
00:08:04,637 --> 00:08:09,379
This is standard physical chemistry, the
kind covered in the American Chemical

137
00:08:09,379 --> 00:08:11,689
Society's general chemistry resources.

138
00:08:11,689 --> 00:08:15,640
A knife cutting a cooler piece is cutting
through thicker liquid.

139
00:08:15,640 --> 00:08:19,466
A second, less certain part of the
explanation is often repeated.

140
00:08:19,466 --> 00:08:24,293
As the outer layers cool, the fibres there
may relax slightly and hold some of the

141
00:08:24,293 --> 00:08:26,941
loose liquid back in the spaces between
them.

142
00:08:26,941 --> 00:08:32,339
The idea is plausible, but it is harder to
isolate in a measurement than viscosity

143
00:08:32,339 --> 00:08:32,536
is.

144
00:08:32,536 --> 00:08:36,427
Treat it as a possible contributor, not a
settled cause.

145
00:08:36,427 --> 00:08:40,040
Either way, both effects push in the same
direction.

146
00:08:40,040 --> 00:08:44,610
A slightly cooler, more evenly heated
piece of meat releases its loose liquid

147
00:08:44,610 --> 00:08:49,357
more slowly when it is cut, so less of it
reaches the board in the first minute.

148
00:08:49,357 --> 00:08:52,808
Put the two effects together and the role
of size becomes clear.

149
00:08:52,808 --> 00:08:57,741
A thick roast stores more heat, so its
centre rises for longer after it leaves

150
00:08:57,741 --> 00:09:01,347
the oven, and its outside and inside start
further apart.

151
00:09:01,347 --> 00:09:05,395
Resting has more work to do on both the
temperature and the liquid.

152
00:09:05,395 --> 00:09:10,850
A thin steak stores little heat, evens out
quickly and cools fast whether you wait or

153
00:09:10,850 --> 00:09:11,107
not.

154
00:09:11,107 --> 00:09:16,471
For a thin steak, the gap between resting
and not resting is small and hard to

155
00:09:16,471 --> 00:09:17,021
measure.

156
00:09:17,021 --> 00:09:21,690
Resting changes a roast more than a thin
steak, which matches what the thermometer

157
00:09:21,690 --> 00:09:23,171
and the scale both report.

158
00:09:23,171 --> 00:09:27,080
When you watch this back, notice that the
two instruments do different jobs.

159
00:09:27,080 --> 00:09:31,283
The probe thermometer answers the
temperature question, and its answer is

160
00:09:31,283 --> 00:09:33,816
clean: the centre keeps rising off the
heat.

161
00:09:33,816 --> 00:09:37,880
The scale answers the juice question, and
its answer is quieter.

162
00:09:37,880 --> 00:09:42,643
Less liquid reaches the board, but some of
the water left by another route.

163
00:09:42,643 --> 00:09:46,932
Watch the sear, which keeps sizzling
because it never sealed anything.

164
00:09:46,932 --> 00:09:51,773
And notice the one box left open, which is
how much resting changes total loss.

165
00:09:51,773 --> 00:09:54,776
The answer depends on the cut and the
conditions.

166
00:09:54,776 --> 00:09:58,781
This evidence does not give a single
correct resting time for every cut.

167
00:09:58,781 --> 00:10:03,750
The only time stated in this episode is
the USDA's 3-minute rest, and that rest

168
00:10:03,750 --> 00:10:06,392
exists for temperature, not for juiciness.

169
00:10:06,392 --> 00:10:10,010
The evidence does not show that juice
migrates to the centre.

170
00:10:10,010 --> 00:10:14,696
It also does not show that resting saves
most of the liquid that cooking pushes

171
00:10:14,696 --> 00:10:19,144
loose, because much of that liquid is gone
before the meat leaves the heat.

172
00:10:19,144 --> 00:10:22,829
It does not settle whether people can
taste the difference in a blind

173
00:10:22,829 --> 00:10:27,837
Juiciness is partly a sensation, and
sensory studies measure people, not water.

174
00:10:27,837 --> 00:10:34,499
Finally, the carryover rise has no fixed
size. Mass, heat and time set its range.

175
00:10:34,499 --> 00:10:37,117
So the minutes after cooking are not
empty.

176
00:10:37,117 --> 00:10:42,171
Heat is still crossing the meat from the
outside in, and the loose liquid inside it

177
00:10:42,171 --> 00:10:43,755
is cooling and thickening.

178
00:10:43,755 --> 00:10:48,000
Resting is the name for letting both of
those finish before the knife decides

179
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:49,157
where the water goes.
